Andrew Gaylard wrote:
> 
> Hi All,
> 
> I'm curious to know whether conventional PCI-based graphics
> boards will work in 'my' Alpha-433au, under DU-4.0E.  The
> system came with a 2MB board, reported as follows:
> 
> vmunix: tga0 at pci0 slot 12
> vmunix: tga0: depth 8, map size 2MB, 1280x1024
> vmunix: tga0: ZLXp2-E, Revision: 34
> 
> This, as you can see, is an 8-bit card.  Am I correct in
> assuming that tga == Trident Graphics Adaptor?
> 
> If I can't replace this board with an 'off-the-shelf' graphics
> board, can I reduce my resolution to, say 1024x768 or 1152x900,
> and use this device in 16bpp mode?
> 
> For those who're curious, there are two palette-intensive apps
> which I can't live without: Netscape and Acrobat.  Both of them
> can be run with private colourmaps, of course, but who would
> want to? (colourmap flashing == headache material)
> 
> Frankly, I can't believe that any system billed as a 'workstation'
> can be shipped with a mere 256 colours in today's world.
> 


  The TGA2 (aka ZLXp2-E, aka PowerStorm 3D30) is a DEC developed
graphics adapter.  It is arguably the fastest 2D option ever built, even
after several years on the market.  The card you have is a 8-plane-only
version (most of the world still does use only 8-planes).  You can buy
the TGA2-E2 or E3 (the PowerStorm 4D20) which has much more memory, and
will do deeper pixel depths, and low-end 3D rendering.  Same chip,
different RAMDAC and more memory.


















