Allen,

Most modems sold today are designed primarily to work with Windows.
You may need to run a utility program provided by the manufacturer to
set it up as COM2.  This is definitely the case if the modem is a PCI
modem.  

Furthermore, some older DOS communications software may not work with
a newer modem, unless the modem exactly emulates the architecture of
an older 8250, 16450, or 16550 serial port.  Older DOS comm software
was often written down and dirty for speed, accessed the COM port
hardware directly... Ben Myers 

