Z.M. Yu (r35961@email.sps.mot.com) wrote:
: I have an VAX workstation installed VAX/VMS 5.4 with two char terminals,
: At present I installed EXCEED S/W on my PC (window NT), the proposal is
: to access openwindow remotely from PC , but I can not find the
: workstation through broadcast.  I am not familiar with open window
: display management on VAX. Could you advise me how to config  and if any
: process need be started to support remote graphics access.

The reason that eXceed does not see the VMS machine through XDMCP
Broadcast is that DECwindows (not "open window" -- that's a SUN thingy!)
does not come with an XDM (X Display Manager). An XDM is bundled with
recent versions of several commercial TCP/IP stacks (e.g. TCPware, Multinet).

Preconditions for the following to work properly are that a) DECwindows
is installed (i.e. not tailored away) and b) TCP/IP installed on the VMS
machine for X Windows to talk to your eXceed.

You have to tell DECwindows that it may utilize TCP/IP as a transport:

Edit SYS$MANAGER:DECW$PRIVATE_SERVER_SETUP.COM (copy from the .TEMPLATE
of the same name if it doesn't exist). Find or add a line

  DECW$SERVER_TRANSPORTS == "LOCAL,DECNET,TCPIP"

in the "Cluster Common ..." section and restart DECwindows by

  @SYS$STARTUP:DECW$STARTUP RESTART

from a sufficiently privileged account. Beware!! All X clients will be
terminated by this!!

Furthermore, the TCP/IP REXEC service has to be up and running. How to
find out whether it does, and what to do if it doesn't depends on your
TCP/IP package.

Create a file X_TCP.COM in your login directory containing

  $ IF P1 .EQS. "" THEN P1 := <your PC's IP address or hostname>
  $ SET DISPLAY /CREATE /TRANSPORT=TCPIP /NODE='P1'
  $ @SYS$MANAGER:STARTSM

Switch eXceed's Transport from "XDMCP Broadcast" to "Passive".

Then, open eXceed's Xstart program. Enter the hostname, user, and password.
Enter "@@SYS$LOGIN:X_TCP.COM @a" as the command (without the quotes).
Clicking "Run!" should now get you a session manager.

If you don't want to switch the transport globally, you can save the Xstart
configuration and use it in an Xsession configuration, where you can configure
the transport locally.

Hope it helps,
  Martin
