>of either SRM
>or ARC. If your machine is one of these then you need to reload the
>firmware
>each time you want to change from SRM to ARC.

Some of the single-Flash-ROM AlphaStations 200 still have the second
socket so you could add the missing ROM yourself. Some don't even have
the socket, and the AlphaStations 255 in general have only one Flash
ROM. If you frequently need to switch between Windows NT and OpenVMS
or Unix, you can load the SRM firmware into the Flash ROM, put ARC on
a floppy and boot from the floppy (>>> boot dva0). Of course it's
slower than from ROM, but then... how often do you reboot?

I believe the procedure to create a bootable ARC floppy is described
somewhere in Digitals/Compaqs ftp archives.


