Congratulations on completely missing the point. I'll spell it out for you.

The point: Of course there are specific instances where getting more
cheaper and slower systems is better than getting fewer expensive and
faster systems. There are also specific instances where the opposite
is true. There are also many instances where it could go either way.
Your one specific case is irrelevant as to whether or not the pricing
on the DP264 systems is too high. Your practice of comparing the cost to
performance ratio of a high end Alpha system (since the thing is the fastest
thing of the sort commercially available it pretty much defines the high end)
to mid-range Intel systems is not valid - you get exactly the same "bad ratio"
comparing your mid-range Intel systems to high-end Intel systems, or any other
high-end system. This is particularly true of brand spanking new ones that
have not been around long enough to undergo the even the first round of price
reductions that all systems eventually go through. Your "benchmark" is about
as meaningful as basing every decision completely on the MHz rating of the
processors or the processor's rating in MIPS - i.e. it is not only not useful,
it is just plain foolish.

