From: reid@psi.ch (Dr Ivan D Reid, muSR Facility)
Subject: Effect of cache memory on Digital PWS500a
Date: 1999/01/23
Message-ID: <slrn7ak04k.ge003a.reid@PSW086.PSI.CH>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 436024918
Followup-To: comp.sys.dec
Organization: Paul Scherrer Institute
Reply-To: Ivan.Reid@PSI.ch
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.lang.fortran

	I've finally been able to satisfy my curiosity about the effect cache
memory would have on my Digital Personal Worksatation 500a.  This machine
has a 500 MHz Alpha processor, 128 MB of main memory and, until today, no
off-processor cache.

	Thanks to the cooperation of Carlson Computers I was able last week
to purchase a used 2 MB cache module and import it at a cost considerably
below the quoted price from Digital in Switzerland.

	I ran a few benchmarks before and after installation; two are
programmes that I use in my research, one is a synthetic matrix-inversion
benchmark that was posted to c.l.f.  All were compiled using Digital Visual
Fotran 5.0.  Results were:

Programme	Problem		Time before	Time after	Time change
MOPAC 6		Perylene[1]	14.50 s		13.25 s		 -9%
Test_fpu[2]	Gauss 2000	 8.2  s		 8.2  s		  0%
		Crout 2000	 5.1  s		 5.0  s		 -2%
		Crout 2		 5.8  s		 3.8  s		-34%
		Lapack 2	 6.5  s		 3.9  s		-40%
		Total		25.5  s		20.9  s		-18%
MINFIT/MINUIT	Migrad[3]	 7.49 s		 6.11 s		-18%
		Minos		20.11 s		20.46 s		 +2%

	Overall, the cache seemed to be an improvement, especially in the
large 501x501 matrix inversions.  Obviously the changes are very programme
and data-size dependent as one would expect.

[1] Perylene is C20H12; time is to converge to a stable configuration starting
from an initial guess.
[2] Posted to c.l.f by David Gemini.
[3] MINFIT is a MINUIT-based fitting programme for our muon-spin spectroscopy
data; problem was fitting a 23-parameter model to 4 7700-bin histograms of
data, starting from a manually derived initial guess.

-- 
Ivan Reid, Paul Scherrer Institute, CH.   http://musr0.psi.ch/  reid@psi.ch



