But often the program does not distribute perfectly. I've a number of
tasks which would run nicely on clusters, provided that the individual
systems in the cluster have at least 256M of RAM - and 256M is pretty
much impossible to put into old boxes and not very cheap for larger
ones.

There's also quite an administrative difference between getting a couple
of medium-sized boxes and a Beowulf - Beowulfs use enough electricity
and space that you have to start accounting for both, and at that point
it's much more convenient to get the medium-sized boxes (the Beowulf
*really* needs a separate office; I've stood in the room with a 16-box
one, and it's singnificantly too loud to work). Two-way SMP boxes are
easy to work with from the console; Beowulfs require scripts to start
applications, and generally are very painful to do anything at all
interactive with.

P2/450 systems exist to run legacy code and serial code at reasonable
speed.

Tom




