($2134), I get 40% of the perf for 25% of the 
price.  I think I can scale it up to more CPUs with similar efficiency.
For $8000, I could get 8 dual P-II/400s, and have a FASTER distributed machine
than the DP264.

>performance machines than yours, and are willing to pay for such performance -
>because it offers them a marked advantage.

>Whether it's deriving a stock trend faster than you - finding oil faster than
>you - processing more catalog sales faster than you - there's almost always a
>solid reason why these "buyers" will purchase the fastest system available
>that'll solve their problems.

Yes, but what if I can achieve faster results for the same money,
by not paying for hardware with bad price/perf?

>Further - and with respect to how much such a buyer is willing to pay for the
>best performance available - the presumption that underlies your sentiments -
>that price/performance should fit a linear scale - is absurd - as even Intel's
>pricing structure for processors should prove (look at the price vs operating
>frequency across any of their processor product lines and tell me that you can
>plot price/performance using a linear function)...

Extra performance is worth SOME premium.  We paid a premium to get
400MHz P-IIs instead of 350MHz P-IIs.  Single-CPU performance *is*
worth a premium, but I don't think it's worth a premium of 4x price/perf,
or even 2x, ESPECIALLY WHEN YOUR PLATFORM ISN'T THE ACCEPTED STANDARD,
which is Intel.  "Hmm, my applications will have to be ported to
this platform, and I have to pay a lot more for this platform, and
the price perf sucks.  Uhhh....  Maybe I'll go with Intel."

For Compaq to make headway with Alpha, they need to bring the price/perf
numbers more competitive with Intel:  it is the only way they can grow
their market much.  Their strategy of charging high premiums, along
with other poor business decisions, has kept alpha tightly into
a niche market:  a niche market which is shrinking as performance
hungry but price-sensitive people like me leave in favor of clusters
of Intel machines.

At $4k for their motherboard + CPUs I would have been tempted.
At $3k, I'd have gone with Compaq.  Below that, and I think they'd
gain market share quickly, and have a chance to make Merced/McKinley
irrelevant.

Perhaps later this year I'll be buying 21264s, if they're more
aggressive about improving price/perf than Intel is.

>Again, let's compare the latest/greatest dual-processor kernels - with
>comparably sized caches - and see where that takes us (even with respect to
>"price/performance" as a side-bar topic). I suspect the similarities are
>closer than what Terry's article would lead the unaware reader...

Compaq's DP264 rules over all else, the undisputed king 
of performance, soundly stomping Intel's high end, even possibly
in price/perf.  Too bad their price/perf is so much worse than
that of  P-II/400 dual configurations.

PeterM




