>
>This is all well and good, but you shoul dnote that you now have a computer
>that is about 50% as fast at integer processing and 20% as fast at floating
>point processing as you could have had.

Sure, there are people who have systems which run at 100% of CPU capacity 
day in and day out, and they will see the performance differences.

However, there are a *lot* of other machines with workloads nothing 
like that.  In many instances, IO times are rate limiting, and the IO
subsystems on the Alpha and the Intel are nowadays not only equivalent, but
often identical.  This applies to both the disks and the ethernet
connections.  So, for many applications (example: web serving of largely
static pages) the extra CPU power won't show up *at all* in the performance
of the machine. 

There are also workloads which do have a significant CPU component, but
where the jobs take only a fraction of a second.  Until the point where the
machine begins to saturates the CPU, the performance from the end user
perspective will be identical.  It's only when such a machine is heavily
loaded that anybody could tell them apart. 

For office use, it's usually a moot point - how many people use even a
fraction of the CPU they've got when they're in Word or Excel?  That's why
AMD ate Intel's breakfast last year - the K6 was more than good enough
for most things, and it cost less.

I still maintain that the Alpha will be forever a niche player unless it
can be sold head to head price competitive at the low and midrange part of
the market.  Given two $1000 machines, you'll usually pick the faster one
(except when there is no software for it, but that's another thread.) Given
a choice between a $1000 Intel/Amd, and even a $15000 Alpha, only the FPU
junkies will choose the latter. 

Regards,

