In article <36A61410.4E6F@mailexcite.com>, Dennis Otero <dr.otero@mailexcite.com> writes:

>Is there some way I can find out how much physical memory the server has
>without askin the system administrator? Take into consideration that no
>tools are available and access to /dev/mem or kmem is not available
>either.

You can use uerf(8) or DECevent (dia(8)) to look at the boot messages.
But, this is generally restricted to root.  You can write a program
to use getsysinfo(2) to query the kernel for the amount of memory,
but that requires a compiler suite.  It seems that new commands are
added all the time, so search through the "Related Information"
sections of suitable manual pages to see if anything is included
in the system to get it.  I had hoped some version of sizer would
print the amount of memory, but it doesn't on V4.0E.

You might look around for some free software that does it.  Monitor
does, but you may need the compiler to build it for your version.
I've heard of something called sysinfo that probably does.  Collect
might.

