My guess is that these systems are well configured for the kinds of jobs
you are doing.  8GB and 8 CPUs is a "sweet spot" for jobs that are
memory bandwidth intensive, as in SPECfp95.  For applications that look 
more like SPECint95, you can keep on sailing right up to 14 CPUs.

Does your 8/8 system look like this in /var/adm/messages?

  Jan  8 12:49:02 leavme vmunix: TLMEM at node 7
  Jan  8 12:49:02 leavme vmunix: TLMEM at node 6
  Jan  8 12:49:02 leavme vmunix: TLMEM at node 5
  Jan  8 12:49:02 leavme vmunix: TLMEM at node 4
  Jan  8 12:49:03 leavme vmunix: Dual TLEP at node 3
  Jan  8 12:49:03 leavme vmunix: Dual TLEP at node 2
  Jan  8 12:49:03 leavme vmunix: Dual TLEP at node 1
  Jan  8 12:49:03 leavme vmunix: Dual TLEP at node 0


That's a classic configuration for the 8/8 sweet spot.  The other thing
to check is whether all four boards are the same size, and whether 

   >>> show interleave

replies 'default'.  If not, set it that way.


Some additional clues on this topic are at 
http://www.digital.com/alphaserver/performance/smp_scaling.html

