Oh yeah, if that's the case, surely you should do that.  I couldn't find
a significant difference (i.e. more than the timing difference from run
to run with our code) between using -O (which really is -O4) and -O5
using DEC Fortran (77) under DEC Unix.

I think Patrick McGehearty hits the bull's eye:

> It may be the codes that Toon uses are already well written to 
> be relatively easy for the compiler to generate good code.  In my role 
> as compiler performance analyst, I regularly see codes where the flags 
> chosen can vary application performance by factors of two or more.

It's probably not so much that the code is written well, as well that
our code has rather large inner loops, which give most compilers ample
opportunity to optimize register usage, scheduling of code and what not,
without having to go to extremes like "software pipelining",
"prefetching" and such.  Furthermore, we hardly use transcendentals, so
Digital's clever 4-sqrts-at-a-time trick gets lost in the noise for our
code; for others this might easily mean a factor of three in speed.

Even simple measures like unrolling do not buy us much [for the few
loops for which it would be beneficial].

HTH,

