	As an aside...

	The 21364 has a L2 load-to-use penalty of 12 ns.  Immediately
	adjacent L2, 15 ns access.  From this diagram:

http://www.digital.com/alphaoem/present/sld019.htm

	At worst (assuming cache line request is routable) the most
	remote cache line is 3 caches away or 3 * 15 ns + load or
	57 ns but there are only 2 of them:

			A B C D
			E F G H
			I J K L

	If H is the accessor , then B and J are 3 traversals away.  So
	average L2 latency would be:
		
		12		      +   ! local
		4 * (( 1 * 15 ) + 12) +   ! adjacent
		5 * (( 2 * 15 ) + 12) +
		2 * (( 3 * 15 ) + 12) / 12

	37 ns on average to access 18 MByte L2 aggregate.  Tremendous.  No
	need for L3, so we start to see really cost effective servers at
	the high-end, especially after a shrink to .13.

	How will this effect Spec?  Will Spec up the cache blow-out to
	prevent 21364 64 CPU configs from skewering things?  I believe 
	average L2 latency to be 72 ns for 64 CPUs in an 8 x 8 grid.  Can we 
	expect 8 x 8 configurations?

	You have danced around the UMA / NUMA issue.  How is this
	Real World defined:

>
> It can be easily conceptualized as have worst case latency no worse than the 
> fully connected crossbar and average latencies that are much better.
> 

	If it can be easily "conceptualized", can you trot out specifics?
	How many CPUs per node?  How many switches?  Latencies.  Near term or 
	long term?  If you can't tell us, please don't tell us you would
	have to shoot us to tell us.  Andrew H. does wear a bit thin at times.
	Thanks.

				Rob




