In article <F5rFp6.IHw@spcuna.spc.edu>, Terry Kennedy <terry@spcunb.spc.edu> wrote:
>In comp.sys.dec John Furcht <johnfurcht@worldnet.att.net> wrote:
>> I have an MVII, that now requires me to enter the date and time on bootup.
>> Is there a battery in these machines that hold the settings after power
>> down?
>
>  Note that if it's remembering the boot device, the problem may not be
>with the battery - could be the clock, or (if you're running VMS) the
>SYSGEN parameter that forces setting the time.
>

If it's remembering the boot device it isn't a MicroVAX II ! The KA630 
implemented a "sniffer" boot (i.e. it poked around looking for something to 
boot) because it had no NVRAM and no console storage to keep a boot device 
name in. The MicroVAX 3 series onwards (KA650, KA655 etc.) did have NVRAM and 
so will remember a boot device setting (except for mine 'coz the batteries are 
dead :-( )

