In article <36a43cc8.0@rzunews1.unizh.ch>, k042240@rzu.unizh.ch (Gideon Hayford Chonia) writes:
>
>I will appreciate very much if someone could help
>me with the boot command for DEC system 5000

I'm not going to shut mine down to verify (up 214 days), so this is
from memory...

The general form of the boot device name is:

tc#[ /device|protocol [/ kernel-name ] ]

The tc# is the TURBOchannel number of the adapter from which
you want to boot.  On the original DECsystem 5000/200, the
built-in SCSI adapter is 5 and the built-in Ethernet adapter
is 6.  For the Model 100 family and 240/260, the built-in
SCSI is 3.  I don't recall where the Ethernet adapter is.

For network adapters you specify a protocol after the tc#/.
Typically this will be mop, but I think the console is
written to also support tftp.

For SCSI devices the "device" is a two letter string for the
device type and the SCSI target ID.  For disks the string is
"rz" and for tapes "tz".  For disks you specify the kernel
name after yet another /.

If you have a CITCA (CI/TURBOchannel adapter) and suitable
console firmware, I think it will understand "ra" for disks
and "tms" for tapes.

Options are specified after the boot device string:

boot 5/rz0/vmunix -a

boot 6/mop

boot 1/tz3

boot 2/rz4/genvmunix
   
