I have a DEC 3000 model 600. It has an "alternate console" serial port.
This port is evidently fixed to exactly 9600bps (and other specifics).
Is there any way to convince the hardware to allow different settings?

(A jumper would be ideal. Extensive use of a hammer would be tempting.)

Having it fixed to one specific set of settings is rather stupid - I can
see having them for a default, or even set to specific settings if the
alternate console switch is thrown, but I fail to see the point of not
making it a normal serial port otherwise. If it is actually not possible
to allow it to be changed somehow, then whoever came up with this bright
idea needs to be smacked upside the head with a large blunt object.

(Yes, I know there's another port that works - that's what would be used
for the *other* thing I'd like to plug into this box. I have two such
things - fluorometers to be specific - which only communicate at 1200bps,
even parity, and suchlike.)

As far as I can tell nobody ever made a serial port card for the
Turbochannel bus. Is this correct?

--- Carl



