steele_b@spiceisle.com wrote:
> 
> In article <1999Jan18.213204.1@eisner>,
> 
> > The hsd10 is so old I doubt it supports 18g drives. I'd even double check on
> > the 9g drives. For that matter, does it even support wide drives??? Check
> > DECs StorageWorks web page for the HSD10 and see what drives are listed as
> > supported before you buy anything.
> 
> Hi Bob:
> 
> After my original post, one or two persons contacted me and said that the
> HSD10 would support both the 9 and 18 GB drives.  A few others said only the
> 9 GB drives are supported.
> 
> I searched the StorageWorks site, and then the whole DEC site, and I finally
> found out (on a "support update" document) that the 9 GB drives are supported
> by the HSD10 if the firmware revision is at C294 (or C296 - can't remember).
> The support document was a bit vague wrt support for the 18GB drives.
> 
> Here's where it gets more interesting.  I checked my HSD10 units, and their
> firmware revisions were at B293 and B475 respectively.  I then contacted my
> DEC support rep, and he said that firmware upgrades were NOT available for
> the HSD10s.  Not satisfied with this answer, I went back to the DEC site, did
> a search on HSD10 and firmware, and guess what - firmware updates for these
> units ARE available and can be downloaded from the website.  So, back to the
> DEC rep I go, armed with this information.  He contacts his DEC support
> person, and they then faxed me a copy of a document showing how to upgrade
> the HSD10's firmware.  The operation was quite painless on the HSD10 at
> revision B475, and I'll be doing the one at B293 this coming weekend.
> 
> The HSD10 has a narrow SCSI i/f, but it does support wide drives when a
> 16-bit i/o "personality" module is used in the StorageWorks shelf.  We're
> using RZ29B- WV drives with our HSD10s without any probs.
> 
> I've placed the order for the 9GB drives - I decided not to take chances with
> the 18GB units :-).
> 
> Brian Steele
> 
> -----------== Posted via Deja News, The Discussion Network ==----------
> http://www.dejanews.com/       Search, Read, Discuss, or Start Your Own


One thing to note with using 18Gb disks and VMS lower than 7.2 is the
cluster sizes. Depending on the nature of the files on the disk (lots of
small files for instance) you can end up with somewhat inefficient use
of space. There are ways around this but it's something to be aware of.

Joe




