I saw that too. I had originally hoped that it was only locked to the
specified settings when acting as an alternate console (since chapter 12
is the "how to set up an alternate console" chapter), but that turned out not
to be the case. I was hoping that there was a jumper on the motherboard that
would remove the brain-dead locks on the port characteristics.

}> (Yes, I know there's another port that works - that's what would be used
}> for the *other* thing I'd like to plug into this box. I have two such
}> things - fluorometers to be specific - which only communicate at 1200bps,
}> even parity, and suchlike.)
}> 
}> As far as I can tell nobody ever made a serial port card for the
}> Turbochannel bus. Is this correct?
}> 
} 
}I've never heard of one. Could your fluorometers be handled via a
}terminal server
}and LAT etc? 
} 
}Joe

Another post pointed to a company that still exists that did make TURBOChannel
serial cards - Magma. Their web site (www.magma.com, oddly enough) does not
list such a product anymore, although they do still have drivers for them for
VMS on their support page (I'm running VMS). Looks like it's time to send
e-mail and see if they still make them.

The terminal server is a possibility (I have a couple of DECserver 200/MC
terminal servers) and I am, in fact, actually using one to talk to a different,
much newer, fluorometer. Part of the problem is that the two older devices
aparently do not do any buffering or flow control - if you are not listening
when they send the data, then that is just too bad. It looks like we may need
to give this a try and see if it will work, hoping that the buffering on the
terminal server is sufficient to ride out any network delays.

It is interesting that DEC eventually decided that forcing all serial
expansion to be done via terminal servers was a stupid idea - there are DEC
serial cards for PCI bus systems (the PBXDA cards) that allow as many as 16
RJ45 serial ports (and up to 64 lines).

--- Carl



