We have a nice Alphaserver 2100A loaded with 4 CPU's as our current main NT
server. For various reasons, we decided to replace it with a new box and
convert it into a Digital Unix based mail server running PMDF (relieving one
of our VMS boxes of that task). We've purchased a Compaq server to be the
new NT server. So, I called DEC to see what it would cost to get a base UNIX
license and 3 SMP Unix licenses to convert the 2100A. First quote was over
$15k. Gee, thats almost as much as I paid for the damned box (through
special offer from DEC about 2 years ago)! Then they remembered to give me
the "educational" discount since we are a university, and the number snuck
below $10k. Oh boy. The reps and their cohorts all said that if I was
trading in teh NT licenses they could do more for me. I informed them that I
still needed those licenses and had bought Compaq hardware to put them on.
"Sorry," they said, "its the best we can do." Sorry indeed. A quick call to
a preferred VAR and I had a quote for the same licenses for a hair over $5k.
Oh, their "used". I have to pay DEC/Compaq a $300 fee to transfer them, but
hey, a $5300 total still smokes $9600. What I don't get is this. My purchase
will put a nice chunk of change in my VAR's pocket, and DEC gets $300. DEC
