H.W. Stockman wrote:
> 
> John Henning wrote:
> >
> [...]
> >    - Optimizations must improve performance for a class of programs
> >      where the class of programs must be larger than a single SPEC
> >      benchmark or SPEC benchmark suite.
> 
> Good rule.  How is it enforced?

In rough order of priority:

1. By technical conscience of the benchmarker and vendor.  I have 
   in the past reminded compiler developers, and they have reminded 
   me, that we must view the benchmarks in the context of their 
   attempts to represent real world programs.

2. By peer review by other technical engineers at other vendors.  
   "What exactly does that switch DO?" we ask each other.  We insist 
   on a written description, with sufficient detail so that we can 
   understand it, prior to the posting of a result.

3. By evolution of the suite.  We tend to have a strong predisposition
   against keeping a benchmark more than 2 "rounds" - that is, if a 
   benchmark was in both CPU92 and CPU95, you are unlikely to see it 
   retained in CPU99.

   The evolution of the suite tends to cause the "benchmark specials" 
   to become useless and/or even negatives, as they waste the time 
   that might be more productively spent on general optimizations.

4. By the concept of base.  Peak shows you what can be achieved if 
   you are willing to profile your code, understand what it does to a 
   machine, and adjust how it is compiled.  If you are not willing to 
   do that, base is a better guideline to the performance that you 
   should expect.  By having a variety of programs in the suite, the 
   switch "-Omaximum" is forced to consider both small, cache-resident
   codes and large, memory-sweepers; both huge-basic-block crunchers 
   and branchy codes that can't run 5 instrunctions in a row without 
   heading off in another direction; both little loops that ought to 
   be identified for unrolling and big loops that ought not; etc.

- John Henning
  Member of the SPEC CPU Subcommittee



