In article <36A11BE4.2061F580@usNOSPAM.ibm.com>,
Greg Pfister  <pfister@usNOSPAM.ibm.com> wrote:
>John McCalpin wrote:
>> There are two major problems:
>> (1) These test cases are a bit small on uniprocessors, and
>>     are completely cache-contained on multiprocessors.  They
>>     are therefore not representative of what most people
>>     who use this sort of parallelism for.
>> 
>>     SPEC99 test cases will be much better in this regard.
>
>Why not solve the problem once and for all by fixing, not the data set
>size, but the amount of *time* that the benchmark should take?  Then,
>obviously, you need rules for how to scale the data to make it take that
>long; but that isn't excruciatingly difficult.

That is fairly often the way computers are actually used -- I call it
"the principle of constant pain tolerance".

John Gustafson has the right approach -- for the most common cases in
scientific and engineering computing (including a fair number of
strategic business analysis applications, too), what is important is how
long it takes to get an answer of a given accuracy, and how much more
accurate per unit time your answer becomes. (Not that I think that HINT
is the be-all and end-all of benchmarking.)

This is, unfortunately, not always the right answer, and it also makes
it very difficult to compare results in ways to which we have become
accustomed.

You are, of course, welcome to join SPEC and argue that this approach
be adopted....
-- 
