> > On Thu, 14 Jan 1999, Terry C. Shannon wrote:
> > 
> > > [...]
> > > The average commercial TLaser configuration contains between 3 and 4
> > > CPUs.
> > 
> > No doubt. However, I just note that of the four 8400's that I am familiar
> > with at Corning, one of them has 8 CPU's and the other three each have 12
> > CPU's. The 8- and one of the 12-'s run Digital Unix (I help look after
> > them; great toys) and the other two 12-'s run VMS. The 8-CPU system has
> > 8GB memory and I can't remember off the top of my head what the others
> > have.
> > 
> 
> Steve,
> 
> If I may ask, what sort of apps are these TLasers running? The three to
> four CPU average figure came direct from OVMS Engineering, by the way.

Terry,

The Digital Unix (4.0D) systems are running variety of commercial packages
such as ansys, fluent, patran and the like. Typical jobs use 1-4 CPUs each
and run for up to a week. We also have a collection of in-house codes
written in C that are for the most part floating-point driven. All are
fiber optic system simulations of one kind or another.  The 8-CPU Tlaser
has 5/625's, and the 12-CPU model has 5/300's. These systems have from
16-18GB of /tmp and about 16GB swap. All o/s filesystems are AdvFS on
dual-shadowed disks using LSM. User files come in over FDDI from a pair of
AlphaServer 2100 systems running ASE 1.5 and DU 4.0D. These two 8400's are
the center of a research-oriented compute farm of about 50 machines that
includes a 7760 and some 2100's, with most of them Alpha workstations
(433au's, 500au's, 600au's). My own personal workstation is a member of
this 'cluster', but it's the slowpoke (a 4/266). We use Platform
Computing's Load Sharing Facility (LSF) and run on average 350 batch jobs
per day; sometimes the queues have -thousands- of jobs waiting. It is all
very stable. There are 100's of disk spindles, and ASE and LSM have
saved our butts several times after disk failures.

One of the VMS systems has 12 5/300 CPU's, and the other has 12 5/625
CPU's. Both run financial applications: PeopleSoft, Oracle, etc. I
installed the O/S (VMS 7.1-1H2) and the Cobol compiler; other than that I
don't have much day-to-day contact with them. 

I *think* that the 12-CPU systems each have 4GB memory.

