>> This criticism is unfair. In order to conclude that, you would have to
>> take a large body of real-world programs, try some of the `peak'
>> optimizations on them, and show that no real-world program benefits
>> from the weird flags that help SPEC benchmark programs. In this
>> example (Compaq DS20), most of the weird flags are for the KAP tool;
>> the Digital Fortran flags are simple things like "-speculate all",
>> "-inline none", "-transform_loops", "-unroll 8".
>
>Although I agree with you in principle, I get really uneasy when just
>adding a set of flags to a compile statement one can get a twofold
>increase in performance (this happens on some of the tests - turb3d, and
>in a lesser way - su2cor).  
>
>This is not something I see in practice.
>
>-- 
>Toon Moene (toon@moene.indiv.nluug.nl)
>Saturnushof 14, 3738 XG  Maartensdijk, The Netherlands
>Phone: +31 346 214290; Fax: +31 346 214286
>g77 Support: fortran@gnu.org; egcs: egcs-bugs@cygnus.com

It may be the codes that Toon uses are already well written to be relatively
easy for the compiler to generate good code.  In my role as compiler
performance analyst, I regularly see codes where the flags chosen can vary
application performance by factors of two or more.  Take the HP flag
"+Odataprefetch". Using means that inner loops will include an additional
pre-load instruction for every 4 or 8 direct loads (32 byte cache lines mean
4 real*8 or 8 real*4 data items per cache line prefetched).  Not using means
the prefetches are not included.  Adding prefetching to a loop who's data is
in cache causes it to run approximately 10% slower.  Failing to add
prefetching to a loop who's data in not in cache causes it to run
approximately 50% faster.  So there is no "optimal for all programs" setting
for the flag.  With a few more similar effects, it is not hard to see that
flags can cause more than a factor of two in application performance.

I maintain a suite of hundreds of performance test loops taken from many
sources.  The average test runs 20 times faster at full optimization than
with no optimization.  Some only show 5 times improvement while others
show even more improvement.  And I'm only talking about the difference
between +O0 and +O3.  Special flags can make even greater differences.
So if you really care about the performance of your application, it pays to
spend some time learning about compiler optimization flags (or paying
someone else to do it for you).  Depending only base optimization flags is
like having a turbocharger built into your car and then disabling it.

- Patrick McGehearty
