Alan Frisbie wrote:
> This is a lesson that DEC/Compaq needs to take to heart: Supporting
> developers at the low end drives sales at the high end.   In those
> days, there was no need to make it especially easy for developers.
> Today, you not only need to make it easy, you must actively attract
> developers.   I don't see this happening in the VMS world, and
> thus Compaq is cutting its own throat as far as VMS systems are
> concerned.

True, but that's only half of the equation.

In order to attract developers, the other half is the *MARKET* for their
software. Why develop software for a potential market of circa. 500,000
(known OpenVMS sites worldwide) when you can develop software on
(another o.s.) with a potential market at least 100 times greater than
that in the U.S. alone?

*THAT* is why affordable VMS license *FOR* *END* *USERS* are so vital!

Ya gotta remember - anyone can get an "IA-32" machine - complete with
o.s. and applications pre-installed - for less than $1,000 U.S. What VMS
system can even come close to that? (Answer: *NONE* !!!) Developers can
get the same machine (note that the licenses never expire) and buy
shrink-wrapped, off-the-shelf development kits for $500 or so (again,
you buy it *ONCE* - the licenses never expire !!!).

VMS needs to get on *THAT* ball - AND STAY THERE !!!

