While I would certainly agree that the 8400 scales poorly, the examples
you chose are not particularly scalable, either.....


Just for general interest ----
====================================================================
What You Can Learn From SPECrate Measurements:

	The single-cpu SPECint95 and SPECfp95 values can be obtained
	from the SPEC_rate95 values by simply dividing the uniprocessor
	SPEC_rate95 by 9.  For the Sun E3002, for example, the
	uniprocessor SPECfp_rate95 is 135, precisely 9 times the
	SPECfp95 value of 15.0.

	A machine that is usefully scalable should have a SPECfp_rate95
	per processor that is both high and almost constant as the
	machine size grows.


Sun UE10000:

	The Sun UE10000 has a SPECfp_rate95 per processor that is
	almost constant as the machine size grows, but it is not a
	competitive number.  For example, at 32 cpus, Sun reports
	SPECfp_rate95 of 3280, which corresponds to a uniprocessor
	SPECfp95 of 11.4 -- hardly appropriate for such an expensive
	system.  Even the SPECint_rate95 is poor --- at 32 cpus, the
	value of 2555 corresponds to a uniprocessor SPECint95 of only
	8.9 !

	(Obviously, claiming that these results are biased because they
	are derived from 32-cpu results would be a mistake, since that
	would be admitting that the 64-cpu machine does not even scale
	effectively to 32 cpus!)

	Scaling to 64 cpus results in minimal further loss, with the
	effective SPECfp95 per cpu dropping to 10.4 and the effective
	SPECint95 per cpu dropping to 8.6.



HP V-class:

	The HP V-class has both problems to a lesser degree -- HP loses
	15% on uniprocessor performance relative to that of the fastest
	system using the same cpu (24.8/28.5) and a slightly larger
	amount due to imperfect scaling.  The V2250 delivers
	SPECfp_rate95 of 2471 on 16 cpus, corresponding to a per-cpu
	SPECfp95 of 17.1 -- a full 40% less than the SPECfp95 of the
	same cpu in the K580 chassis, and corresponding to a speedup of
	only 11x on 16 cpus.

	The HP V2500 should have slightly higher single-cpu
	performance, and higher per-cabinet performance, but the
	SPECfp_rate95 speedup ratio on 32 cpus will not be good.  Given
	the smaller caches of the PA-8500 there will be significantly
	higher demand on the memory bandwidth, which is already clearly
	inadequate.  As a first guess, I expect a speedup of only about
	14x on 32 cpus for SPECfp_rate95.  SPECint_rate95 numbers have
	already been released -- 7429 on 32 cpus corresponds to a
	single-cpu SPECint95 of 25.8.  The system is obviously not
	broken with respect to this metric!



Compaq/DEC 8400 Servers:

	The 8400 series servers have single-cpu performance degradation
	and scalability issues even for the 21164/EV5 processor line.  
	The single-cpu SPECfp95 of 20.8 at 612 MHz is 5% *lower* than
	the SPECfp95 of the 4100 series box at 533 MHz, despite the 15%
	higher clock speed.  (Note that you can't compare the 8400 to
	the 600 MHz 4100 series machine, since the cache on that 4100
	model is much larger.)

	SPECfp_rate95 speedup the 8400-5/625 on 12 cpus is only 7.3x,
	which is fairly embarassing.  With the faster EV6 processors,
	the the 8400-6/575 has a SPECfp_rate95 speedup of only 4.2x on
	8 cpus, and an equivalent single-processor SPECfp95 of 24.9 per
	cpu.   Although this is an excellent number, it does not leave
	the competition behind -- as the uniprocessor numbers would
	initially suggest.



SGI Origin2000 Servers:

	For the SPECrate95 benchmarks, the Origin2000 experiences
	bandwidth contention only within each 2-cpu node.  The 250 MHz
	system shows a SPECfp_rate95 speedup of 1.77x on 2 cpus (on the
	same node), and the speedup is subsequently linear in the
	number of nodes applied, until you get to really high processor
	counts where some of the peculiarities of the run rules for
	SPECrate start to cause some bottlenecks.

	For example, at 32 cpus (16 nodes), the SPECfp_rate95 is 376
	per node -- only a 4% degradation per node from the single-node
	result.  At 128 cpus (64 nodes), the SPECfp_rate95 per node is
	351, corresponding to a single-cpu SPECfp95 of 19.5, which is
	80% of the uniprocessor performance, and corresponds to a
	system throughput speedup of 102x on 128 cpus.  The scalability
	is a bit better for SPECint_rate95, giving 112x on 128 cpus.
