Correct, data placement is all local.  This is one of the more serious
shortcomings of the SPECrate95 benchmarks.  (The other serious
shortcoming is the homogeneity of the workload.)


>So, I was wondering if I am correct in the page allocation strategy
>and what the numbers look like if you do a random or farthest neighbor
>page allocation?  

Actually, I don't know the answer for SPECfp_rate95, because it 
never seemed like a very clever thing to do!  :-)    It is an
interesting idea for a test case....


>You probably can't give specific numbers, but even a general
>qualitative analysis would be interesting.

There are a couple of related measures that I can give.

    Uncontested read bandwidth degrades about 45% when going
    "corner to corner" in a 128-cpu machine (this requires
    traversing 6 routers).  

    STREAM bandwidth drops about 15% when one argument is
    3 routers away, and it drops 15%-20% when both arguments
    are 2 routers away.

    An optimally coded transpose routine (with a 1-D data
    decomposition) runs at about 60% of the rate of a
    parallel (local) copy of the same amount of data.  This
    is perfectly scalable to at least 64 cpus --- I have not
    tried it on more.

    SPECfp95 codes slow down between 1% and 12% when the 
    data is moved to be 2 routers away.  The 12% is for the
    high-bandwidth codes (tomcatv and swim) -- the others
    show less than 10% slowdowns for this case.  (These are
    uncontested results, but contention is pretty minimal
    even for the high-bandwidth codes, since the link
    utilization is less than 25% due to each instance of 
    tomcatv or swim.)

Overall, I think that the Origin2000 is an excellent compromise between
"flatness" and cost for a NUMA system.
