John McCalpin wrote:

> Correct, data placement is all local.  This is one of the more serious
> shortcomings of the SPECrate95 benchmarks.  (The other serious
> shortcoming is the homogeneity of the workload.)

But SPECfp95 parallelized over multiple CPUs represents a harder 
problem, one which only not all have been brave enough to
attempt.  In fact, only 4 companies have submitted results for
it.  A fetch of all www.spec.org SPECfp95 results with 
# CPU > 1 at yields:   

              Highest    Highest        Date of
              #cpu       Result         most recent
 Company      tested     (/#cpu)        Result 
 ----------   ------     ----------     -------
 Compaq/DEC     8        114    (8)     Jan-99
 HP            16         24.8 (16)     Mar-98
 SGI           32         76.6 (16)     Nov-98
 Sun           12         40.8  (4)     Nov-98

The fact that SGI has submitted the most aggressive configurations,
with up to 32 CPUs, speaks well of Origin.

The fact that the AlphaServer DS20, with 2 CPUs, can nearly match
the 16-CPU SGI Origin with 16 CPUs, speaks well of both EV6 and
the DS20 memory system:

   System                         cpus  SPECfp95
   ------------------------------ ----  --------
   SGI Origin2000 16x 250MHz R10k  16    76.6
   Compaq AlphaServer DS20          2    76.1

For more information on the 3 kinds of system load represented by
3 different ways of running the SPEC CPU95 suite, see 
http://www.digital.com/alphaserver/performance/smp_scaling.html



