In article <369F4EA9.862F4F4E@zko.dec.com>,
John Henning  <NhOeSnPnAiMng@zko.dec.com> wrote:
>John McCalpin wrote:
>
>> Correct, data placement is all local.  This is one of the more serious
>> shortcomings of the SPECrate95 benchmarks.  (The other serious
>> shortcoming is the homogeneity of the workload.)
>
>But SPECfp95 parallelized over multiple CPUs represents a harder 
>problem, one which only not all have been brave enough to
>attempt.  

There are good reasons and bad reasons for this....

It is a good thing that autoparallelizing SPEC is harder to do,
so this test does tell something about the vendors' compiler
technologies.

There are two major problems:

(1) These test cases are a bit small on uniprocessors, and 
    are completely cache-contained on multiprocessors.  They
    are therefore not representative of what most people 
    who use this sort of parallelism for.  

    SPEC99 test cases will be much better in this regard.

(2) The test cases are constrained by a "no source code change"
    requirement.  Very few people who do high-performance 
    parallel programming do so in an environment where they
    are not even allowed to add directives/pragmas.  As a
    consequence of the restriction, vendors are encouraged
    to go to extremes to make optimizations that work for 
    this particular set of benchmarks, when the better solution
    would be to just require a directive/pragma and spend
    the effort to develop better functionality.

    I do not know if the SPEC folks have decided exactly
    what approach to take to automagic parallelism in SPEC99.
-- 
