Toon Moene <toon@moene.indiv.nluug.nl> writes:

> Another look at these results (and especially the relatively large
> difference between base and peak SPECfp95) is to note that this
> benchmark set is nearing the end of its useful life.
> 
> Apparently so much is nowadays known about the way to have compilers
> spit out good/better/best code for these ten programs, that only the
> base result is relevant for real world comparison.

This criticism is unfair. In order to conclude that, you would have to
take a large body of real-world programs, try some of the `peak'
optimizations on them, and show that no real-world program benefits
from the weird flags that help SPEC benchmark programs. In this
example (Compaq DS20), most of the weird flags are for the KAP tool;
the Digital Fortran flags are simple things like "-speculate all",
"-inline none", "-transform_loops", "-unroll 8".

I suspect that you'll find that each flag does help some other
programs.

Does Compaq provide a tool which searches through the KAP args to
figure out which weird flags work best for it?

-- gerg



