>mccalpin@frakir.engr.sgi.com (John McCalpin) writes:
>
>
>> There are a couple of related measures that I can give.
>> 
>>     Uncontested read bandwidth degrades about 45% when going
>>     "corner to corner" in a 128-cpu machine (this requires
>>     traversing 6 routers).  
>> 
>Do you know/can you say whether this is because of the bandwidth
>limits in the network or an issue were the number of external
>references reaches the max allowed?  

The bandwidth is strictly limited by latency tolerance here.
The links are each at least as fast as any single node's memory
interface.




>> Overall, I think that the Origin2000 is an excellent compromise between
>> "flatness" and cost for a NUMA system.
>
>I agree, the Origin is certainly a nice machine.  I would be well
>served to have higher internode bandwidth and low internode latency,
>but overall it does a good job.

And of course it would be better if it were free, too, but that 
is not a useful engineering guideline!  Actually, the Origin does 
not need higher inter-node bandwidth -- it has more than the 
CPUs can effectively use already.   Lower latency would help 
the utilization of this bandwidth, of course. 





>Which brings me to another topic that has been rolling around in my
>head since reading Greg Pfister's In Search of Clusters, V2.0.  In the
>book Greg makes the distinction that NUMA is by nessesity less easier
>to program for than an UMA.  

Although there are aspects of this statement that are true, I think 
that most people draw inappropriately broad conclusions from it.

Does it take into account that the extra effort of programming the NUMA
system may provide significantly higher performance than the UMA system
of the same price?   Consider that on the NUMA machine the local
latencies are significantly lower, the local sustainable bandwidths are
higher, the aggregate performance is less subject to contention, and
the NUMA system has more cpus at the same price.

It is easy to get confused about the desirability of an UMA machine ---
try pricing an Origin2000 against a Sun UE10000 sometime.  The price per
cpu difference is shocking -- a 16-cpu Sun UE10000 can easily be >2x 
the list price of a 16-cpu Origin2000.



>Well, this brought up the idea that in
>the not so distant future it should very well be easier to design a
>NUMA system that has at least lower average latency and higher average
>bandwidth than an UMA SMP at a given size.

I have done this.  :-)


>But I see in the future the possibility of having a NUMA machine that
>would have less MAX latency than a UMA machine of a given size.

I think that it depends on how big the system is.  Snoopy systems
have latencies that are roughly proportional to machine size, while
NUMA systems have latencies that are proportional to a root of
the machine size (for grids) or the log of the machine size (for 
hypercubes), and it is inevitable that the snoopy systems will at
some point pass the directory-based system in max latency.
Whether someone would build such a large snoopy system is the 
real issue.....
