In article <77hcmu$8vn@news.or.intel.com>, tsmurphy@ichips.intel.com (Terence Murphy) wrote:
>Hi,
>
>I was wondering about getting Windows NT running on an Alpha system.
>The CD I have ("Windows NT Workstation 4.0") has directories for
>multiple architectures (Intel, Alpha, etc.) and worked on my Intel
>hardware, but is it actually bootable on anything besides Intel?  Or is
>there a special CD for Alpha?  (I have a couple of EV4 systems ... would 
>rather not put Linux on both of them).
>
>If not, how does one obtain an NT/Alpha media?  Do I need an additional
>license as well?

The same distribution medium is used for all architectures.  You don't 
need another disk.  What you do need is to switch your Alpha from SRM 
(Unix & VMS) console to ARC.  Check your hardware manuals on how to do 
it.  Not all alphas have firmware for both consoles.


