        At DECUS '98 in  L.A.,  when  the  subject of the VMSstation was
    raised  in one of the sessions, I was pointed to Ann McQuaid (yes, I
    have her card,  it  says  "Product  Line  Manager,  OpenVMS  Systems
    Group").   I  asked  specifically  about price, but she wouldn't say
    (not too  suprising).   I  said,  essentially,  "it  better be under
    $5000", but she replied that it would be more.  I took that to be "a
    bit more", not "who are you kidding, Mac, it'll be _twice_ that!".

        To be fair, I  _wasn't_  wearing  and suit...  Nevertheless, she
    didn't  seem to want to give me the time of day, let alone listen to
    my arguments for an _affordable_ VMS desktop...which I imposed  upon
    her anyway...  :-(

        As many of you know, a  lot of institutions' purchasing policies
    have  a  break at about $5000.  It is much, _much_ easier to  buy  a
    workstation for $4995, than it is to buy one for  $5099...let  alone
    $9995.  Why doesn't Compaq/Digital know this???

        Oh, and my bid is that the VMSstation will start, bare-bones, at
    about $6125, but  will  cost  just  over  $10K  once  you've added a
    keyboard and mouse, :-) that is, once you've added sufficient memory
    to make it useful, plus a graphics adapter and monitor.

