Marcus Thiessel wrote:
> 

> Compaq had the same problem with the 21264 @ 525Mhz. In September they
> only got 45.2 SPECfp which was a little bit disappointing. Recently
> they publish 58.7 SPECfp where they used a 21264 @ 500Mhz.

And here's why (the following is extracted from the lead article in the
January 10, 1998 Shannon Knows Compaq newsletter. Selected back issues
and other goodies posted at http://www.acersoft.com)...

According to results Compaq recently posted at the SPEC website, the
low-end Goldrush trounces the TurboLaser, even though the latter system
costs more than ten times as much as an entry DS20. Data found at the
Standard Performance Evaluation Corporation's website at
www.specbench.org/ osg/cpu95/results/res99q1/ reveals that the DS20
delivers 27.7SPECint95 and 58.7SPECfp95 of uniprocessor performance. The
benchmark was conducted last month on a DS20 system equipped with a
500MHz 21264 processor with 4MB of L3 cache, 512MB of memory, and
Digital UNIX V4.0E. 

SPEC-tacular Performance

The low-end DS20 virtually equals the 27.8SPECint95 performance reported
for the Compaq AlphaServer GS140. Even more impressive is the fact that
the DS20, at 58.7SPECfp95, bests the GS140 enterprise server's floating
point performance of 45.2SPECfp95 by ~30 percent. Not only does the DS20
offer performance consistent with the EV6 estimates released a year ago,
the ~$23K configuration trounces a system costing well over ten times as
much!

The DS20 results were obtained in January 1999 with the Digital Fortran
V5.2 compiler with the KAP Fortran V4.1 preprocessor, while the GS140
results were obtained in September 1998 with older compilers. A
discussion with a Compaq engineering VP revealed that EV6 performance is
highly dependent on compiler tuning. The DS20 SPECfp results reflect the
next round of Fortran compiler optimizations relative to the GS140
results previously published.

Of equal importance, the DS20 results reflect the low-end server's very
low latency memory subsystem and Tsunami crossbar architecture. Since
SPECfp is extremely sensitive to memory performance, the DS20 Goldrush
validates the design of current and forthcoming crossbar-based servers
from Compaq.

Terry Shannon
Consultant and Publisher, Shannon Knows Compaq




