Terry C. Shannon wrote:

> EV6 performance is highly dependent on compiler tuning. 
...
> Of equal importance, the DS20 results reflect the low-end server's very
> low latency memory subsystem and Tsunami crossbar architecture. 

Hi Terry.  It's certainly true that recompiling with the latest
compilers 
is A Good Thing To Do - that's why I posted the software recommendations 
a few days ago as article

   "21264 software (was: re: Compaq AlphaServer DS20 Performance
Results"

But the bigger reason why the DS20 flies on SPECfp95 is its phenomenal 
single-CPU memory bandwidth.  Compare the DS20 to the best single-CPU 
results from HP, Sun, IBM, SGI as posted through yesterday at 
http://www.cs.virginia.edu/stream/standard/Bandwidth.html

  Machine ID         ncpus COPY   SCALE  ADD   TRIAD

  Compaq_DS20           1 1077.0 1244.0 1179.0 1323.0
  Compaq_AS_GS140-6-525 1  524.7  700.0  615.0  817.4

  HP_C180               1  262.3  262.3  244.9  242.4
  IBM_RS6000-591        1  711.1  695.7  750.0  800.0
  SGI_Origin_2000-250_1 1  332.0  343.0  357.0  358.0
  Sun_UE_6001           1  281.0  281.0  224.0  261.0

Terry also wrote:
> the ~$23K configuration trounces a system costing well over ten times as
> much!

The word "trounce" may be a tad unfair to the GS140.  Yes, the DS20 has
the
phenomenal bandwidth, but the GS140 does pretty well in the table above,
too,
and its SPECfp95 rating of 45.2 is nowhere near to being matched by any 
non-Alpha competitor.  

Remember, the GS140 design center is a larger SMP system, with up to 14
CPUs; 
therefore, it operates to some different constraints than the DS20,
which is 
a new design that is optimized to deliver screamer bandwidth to 1 or 2
CPUs.

  - John Henning
    Compaq
    SPEC CPU95 & McCalpin Streams Benchmarker



