Hello,

John Henning wrote:
> Remember, the GS140 design center is a larger SMP system, with up to 14
> CPUs; therefore, it operates to some different constraints than the DS20,
> which is a new design that is optimized to deliver screamer bandwidth to 
> 1 or 2 CPUs.
I agree that the machines are designed for different workloads. But two
observations: 

1) AFAIK very few customers if any (please correct me if I'm wrong) use 
   more than 10 CPUs in the 8400. You have to pull out memory 
   boards if you want to use more than 10, and the backplane has a hard time
   feeding all these hungry CPUs. So the 14 is more of a physical limit,
   the practical limit seems to be 10.

2) CPQ needs to overhaul the 8400 completely, and soon. The advantage of
   the fast 21264 CPUs is balanced by the lack of SMP scalability compared
   to the E10k or the newest V-Class.

Cheers,
   Henrik

