In article <369ABFC8.4F8@world.std.com>, "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>
>Fine, because the AMD K7 and Alpha will be slot (pin, actually)
>compatible. Hence K7-inside systems can easily be upgraded to Alpha. And
>Alpha can capitalize on the economies of scale inherent in high-volume
>K7 motherboards.
>

Dream on Terry.  So you swap out the CPU, and now you have a system which
can't boot from its own ROM.  So you need to replace the ROM first,
assuming that a BIOS exists for the Alpha for that specific motherboard
(unlikely for most motherboards), and that it is small enough to fit into
the existing ROM.  Then you close up the case, boot it, and the power
supply fails or the unit overheats. 

K7->Alpha upgrades are not going to be common.  Samsung or Compaq might
make dual use motherboards (nobody else will though), but I expect that
they will be factory configured for one processor or the other, and will go
into machines with markedly different prices.  At the very least, expect
the power supply and ventilation capacities on the Alphas to be much larger
than for the K7s.

Knowing Digital, er Compaq, it wouldn't surprise me if the K7 motherboards
which were shipped were intentionally configured so that they could NEVER
accept an Alpha CPU.  Remember, these are the same folks who brought us
epoxy in the backplane and NT only Alphas.

Regards,

