In article <369B8447.51B1@world.std.com>, 
"Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:

>> DEC was not happy at all with
>> Charlie's Backplane Epoxy Remover, or with the third-parties who offered
>> glueless replacement backplanes for the VAXstation II/RC!
> 
> And to give credit where credit is due, The Case of the Epoxy Backplane
> was solved thanks to alien intervention. Some guy from a Flying Disk...

Some of you may remember how I would (at DECUS symposia) get in
line at the mike after every DEC product introduction and ask the
one question that they absolutely didn't want to have asked.   There
was almost always some flaw that they hoped nobody would notice, but
I always seemed to spot it by reading between the lines of the
announcement.

I remember well the announcement of the VAXstation-II.   As usual,
I got in line at the mike.   The DECies saw me.   They (as they
told me later) were more worried than usual.   They thought that
the product was as perfect as they could make it, and couldn't
figure out what flaw I had spotted.   Their pride and joy was
(they thought) about to be roasted.

When I got to the head of the line, I told them, "I think this
product is the greatest thing I have seen from DEC in years.   I
will be buying one as soon as possible."   And I meant every word.

It turned out that I had to wait almost a year until the introduction
of the VAXstation-II/RC (Resin Cured) before I could afford one.
It has served me well for the last 13 years.   In fact, I am using
it *RIGHT* *NOW* to compose this reply.   It is my e-mail and Usenet
machine, even though I use an Alpha for most real work these days.

Yes, the backplane was swapped out a few weeks after I got it.
DEC removed the backplane from the price book just a few days
after Terry's, er, Charlie's story hit the streets.   They didn't
like the idea of people turning a $15,000 product into a $25,000
product with a $300 backplane.   :-)

