>>> DEC was not happy at all with
>>> Charlie's Backplane Epoxy Remover, or with the third-parties who offered
>>> glueless replacement backplanes for the VAXstation II/RC!

> Yes, the backplane was swapped out a few weeks after I got it.
> DEC removed the backplane from the price book just a few days
> after Terry's, er, Charlie's story hit the streets.   They didn't
> like the idea of people turning a $15,000 product into a $25,000
> product with a $300 backplane.   :-)

Judging from me e-mail this morning, some people are not familiar
with this story.   Here it is.

When the VAXstation-II was introduced, the least expensive
configuration (in a BA23 box) was priced at $25,000.   Other 
vendors soon came out with workstations that undercut that price,
but they had little or no expandability.

DEC responded by designing the VAXstation 2000.   However, it was
running behind schedule and DEC badly needed something to sell in
the $15,000 price range.   They couldn't cut the price of the
highly-expandable VAXstation-II, because it cost too much to
manufacture.   They were willing to take a short-term loss, but
that was unacceptable.   They were stuck.

Then some bright person (I wish I knew who) got the idea of making
a non-expandable version of the VAXstation-II that would fill the
sales gap until the VAXstation 2000 was ready.   The way they made
it non-expandable was simple: fill the unused backplane slots with
epoxy glue!   Oh yes, they also changed the plastic badge on the
front of the cabinet and added an extra megabyte of memory.   For
this, they charged $15,000 instead of $25,000.   It was called
the VAXstation-II/RC.   They claimed it stood for "Restricted
Configuration", but we all know that it really stood for "Resin
Cured".   :-)

DEC was *very* quiet about the exact difference between the regular
VAXstation-II and the /RC version.   It took a lot of careful
study (and reading between the lines) of the spec sheets to make
the conclusion that they were one and the same.   I bought one and
disassembled it the day it arrived.   When I saw the epoxy, I had
a laughing fit!   Then I called Terry, er, Charlie (after ordering
a replacement backplane).

When the 2000 was announced, the /RC was quietly retired.

In closing, I would like to thank DEC for selling the /RC.   By
swapping the backplane, I obtained the VAX that I needed to do
the work my customers wanted.   That work drove the sales (to
my customers) of many more VAXen, so everyone profited.   Any
loss they suffered on my system was recovered many-fold on the
sales of big systems to my customers.

This is a lesson that DEC/Compaq needs to take to heart: Supporting
developers at the low end drives sales at the high end.   In those
days, there was no need to make it especially easy for developers.
Today, you not only need to make it easy, you must actively attract
developers.   I don't see this happening in the VMS world, and
thus Compaq is cutting its own throat as far as VMS systems are
concerned.

-- 
