otto wrote in message <369B1005.2130@erols.com>...
>12JAN99
>
>  Just picked up a MicroVax II.  2 hard drives, 1 Tape, 16
Asysnc ports,
>  Ethernet, 1 mem ckt bd. this has VMS on it and it works.
>  Is this system of any value other than an end 70 lb. end
table?
>
How much will someone pay for it?  Think in terms of how
much you would pay for a really nice, fully-loaded 286 based
Netware 2 PC server, I'd say the monetary value for a
commercial user is about the same.  I'm sure the used DEC
parts dealers are using MV IIs for landfill these days, or
just busting up the systems for cards to inventory as spare
parts.

Now for a collector striving to preserve computer history, a
good running MVII is a nice way to keep a VMS system in the
collection.  But there is no shortage of old MV II systems,
so a collector won't pay much either, maybe $100 if you have
a valid license with documentation.  If you don't have the
license PAKs the value goes way down.

By way of comparison, I have a nice loaded MV 3500 (3x the
speed of a MV II) with CMD SCSI controller (probably the
best Q-bus SCSI ever!!), ethernet, rack mount, VT console,
TK50, 32MB, originally a $40K system, got it for the effort
to haul it away from the customer's site after an Alpha
upgrade.  It came with a 15 user VMS 5.5 license too, even
have the PAK and TK50 VMS distribution.  So you're dealing
in a market where the other sellers often give it away for
scrap.
     Jack Peacock




