Terry C. Shannon wrote:
> Yes, it is lower than predicted. DEC initially claimed ~40SPECint95 and
> ~60SPECfp95 for a 600MHz 21264. They clearly fell a bit short on the
> prediction.
> 
> Ah well. Alpha is still faster than Merced(did somebody say McKinley?).
> 
> On the integer side, the 27.7 from a 500MHz chip compares quite nicely
> with 27.9 SPECint from a 525MHz chip. Of couse, the Fortran KAP
> preprocessor and code-profiling tools help, too.

I think someone else already pointed out that the original 
estimate was 30/50, which has been seen.  The later estimate 
was 40/60, but that marketeer was making poorly founded 
assumptions and has been sent to the mines of Moria for 
re-education.

Re: faster than Merced/McKinley - perhaps, but it's hard to 
compare a currently shipping system versus future systems.  
It may be more useful to notice that the 21264 has first 
place, on both SPECint95 and SPECfp95, when comparing current 
vs. current at www.spec.org

Re: "unannounced" - Trust Terry to spot the SPEC submission 
prior to the press event.  The usual pattern is to not submit 
the results until the official announcement day, but this 
time the Product Manager asked that the results be submitted 
in late December, so that they would already be at www.spec.org 
when the press event is held.  I'm not sure whether we'll ever 
do it this way again, there seems to be some tension between 
"getting the word out" vs. "stealing the thunder" from the 
press event.

Re: KAP, profiling, and software in general - the software 
teams have done their usual superb job to ensure smooth support 
for the 21264 while not compromising support of previous Alpha 
processors.  I will attach at the bottom of this note some 
pointers to the latest software.

KAP is used only for SPECfp95, performing optimizations such as 
array padding and parallel decomposition, which then uses f90's 
OpenMP support.  

Profiling is currently used only on the SPECint95 suite.  It is 
interesting that with the 21264, compiler feedback has turned 
out to be more frequently useful than om feedback (om compresses
the image, and is run post-link time):

   go          om feedback
   m88ksim     compiler feedback
   gcc         both
   compress    compiler feedback
   li          compiler feedback
   ijpeg       compiler feedback
   perl        compiler feedback
   vortex      both

For previous Alpha submissions, om feedback was more important 
and compiler feedback was less important.  A way to look at this 
is that for an out-of-order processor, it becomes more important 
to let the compiler know how to arrange the code so that the 
frequently executed sections are more likely to be speculatively
issued by the hardware; and with a 64KB L1 Icache instead of 
the 8KB on the 21164, the code squishing done by om is somewhat 
less important.  

- John Henning
  SPEC CPU guy
  Compaq

Attachment: release note on software versions

------------------

Alpha systems that use the 21264 chip (also known as "ev6") will often
obtain higher performance if the latest compilers are used, together
with the following switches (on Digital UNIX):

    -tune ev6: generate instruction sequences to improve performance on
               ev6 systems; but avoid instructions that were unavailable 
               on earlier processors

    -arch ev6: generate instruction sequences that are optimal for ev6,
               including instructions that were not available on 
               earlier processors

The best 21264 performance is typically observed with "-arch ev6", but
if a program will be used on a variety of system types, you should
either:

   1) use -tune ev6 and do not use the -arch switch 
or
   2) compile two versions of the program, one with -arch ev6 and the
      other without the -arch switch.  

The recommended versions of the C, C++, Fortran, and KAP Fortran
compilers for 21264 systems with Digital UNIX V4.0E, and their
locations, are as follows:

     DEC C V6.0-007              UNIX V4.0E, CMPDEVALT subset (see
below)
     DIGITAL C++ V6.1-027        UNIX layered product CD, October 1998
     Digital Fortran 77 V5.2     UNIX layered product CD, January 1999
     Digital Fortran 90 V5.2     UNIX layered product CD, January 1999
     KAP Fortran V4.1            UNIX layered product CD, January 1999

The Digital UNIX operating system distribution includes two versions of
the DEC C compiler:

    command     subset name      
    -------     ------------      
     cc         OSFCMPLRSnnn      
     cc.alt     CMPDEVALTnnn      

Both versions of the compiler are fully supported by Digital.  The "cc"
version uses slightly older software components.  The "cc.alt" version
uses a newer code generator and will usually provide faster run-time
performance.   Performance improvements provided in "cc.alt" will be
incorporated into later versions of "cc".

The OSFCMPLRS subset may be found on the first CD in the UNIX V4.0E set.
It is typically installed automatically, since it is used for kernel
builds.  The CMPDEVALT subset is on one of the additional CDs that ship
with Digital UNIX V4.0E.

For more information about compilers, see:
    http://www.unix.digital.com/developerstoolkit
    http://www.digital.com/allconnect/cplusplus.htm
    http://www.digital.com/fortran/
    http://www.digital.com/hpc/software/kap.html



