there a
NetBSD/Vax driver for them).

First up is a DTC dual height q-bus card, pulled from an (alledged)
working 11/73 where it was connected to an adaptec scsi/mfm bridge.   In
marker-pen on the top edge near the 50-pin header, it's marked ASSY NO
D1, MODEL 11-1, S/N 5112, in the etch, it's marked FAB. #007A-00003 REV.
03.  The whole board looks like SSI and MSI TTL (74LS...), no large
packages at all.  Two packages have a sticker on them, reading "LSH1" at
position D2 and "LSD0" at position E1.  There's two eight-position DIP
switches as well, at D8 and f3, set to 00001100 and 10100011
respectively. The datecodes on the chips are all 1983.  Apparently they
ran out of real-estate during design because the usual plastic pull
handles are missing and there's no room to mount even one.

Next up is another dual height q-bus card. a sticker on a large IC reads
"TD SYSTEMS INC., LOWELL, MA - USA" and below that, typed "VIK/QT0
#2431".  Also on a 27512 is a sticker reading "Viking Q/B A3.1".
There's a Z80B, a Z80-CIO (Z8536), a WD33C93, an HY6264 (8Kx* RAM, for
the Z80, no doubt), a pair of IDT SAI00P, and a mess of MSI TTL.  The
etch reads "211 00031 REV E" and the silkscreen reads "(C) 1987 Made in
USA ASSY 210 00031 REV".  There's a single red LED next to the 50-pin
connector, and there's plastic pull handles, as is typical.  Between E3
and E4 there's a row of Berg jumpers, labeled 1,2,3,4, with a header on
1,2,4.  Most of the date codes on the chips are 1988, altho some are as
old as 1982.  This was found in a MVII (bought at the local junk
dealer) which had already been scavenged (no drives at all) by someone
else before I got to the scene - although they left a KLESI (M7740), DEQNA
(M7502),
