Digital alone could not afford its own Alpha. Heck, Palmer didn't want to
admit that Alpha was costing Digital hundreds of millions per year because
Digital was not able to use its FAB and
failed to sell production capacity to other chips (except StrongArm). So it
sold it to Intel.

So, one barrier to low volumes was removed by outsourcing all of the
fabrication. But to remain competitive with armies of Intel engineers, Compaq
will have to continue to spend magabucks on R&D to keep Alpha ahead of Intel.
And at the way Intel has been narrowing the gap with Alpha (especially in its
marketing claiming to have the fastest chips around), Compaq will have to
spend enough money to maintain a SIGNIFICANT performance edge to keep Alpha alive.

And the lower the volume, the harder it will be to make money.

As far as I am concerned, for as long as Compaq will be producing machines
that cannot boot all operating systems that can run on Alpha, Alpha has no
future. How can they be so stupid ? Compaq would not think of producing an
Intel PC today that could not run NT since it was designed for low end DOS.
(Granted, some models may be slow or have insufficent memory, but they do not
have hardware design that prevents booting NT).

And as far as NT running on Alpha, I am not sure that even at equal price, the
superior speed of Alpha would make up for the lack of native apps. The day I
start to see mail-order magazines with Alpha PCs running NT that are proce
competitive with Intel, *AND* the day that I start to see mail-order-magazines
with alpha or Intel versions of software is the day I will know Alpha has made it.

Until then, it will remain a niche chip whose odds of survival are low because
of low volumes.

