Jeff Killeen wrote:
> decision that was made was to extend what the PDP-11 did well and not try to
> break new ground trying to turn into VAX2.  It does raise concern when OVMS
> fans seem to promote the concept that OVMS should break new ground and chase
> the market rather than focus on rounding out its functionality where OVMS's
> strengths exists.


The problem is that VMS doesn't have any "particular" strengths. Its strength
is in being a general purpose operating system of HIGH QUALITY AND
RELIABILITY. It does not have any particulr niches in which there is a rich
supply of healthy software.

Tandem has a couple of very focused niches with very specialised software
where it is next to impossible for users to migrate because their requirements
require the use of apps available only on Tandem. (or sometimes only on Tandem
or IBM mainframes at higher price). Credit card processing and transaction
routing (as well as debit card) comes to mind.

I consider VMS to be a superset of RSX-11 (PDP-11). So a migration from PDPs
to VAX was considered (for many) an "upgrade". Some stayed with PDP-11 because
it was better and cheaper for specific applications for which its lower
performance was quite sufficient (manufacturing for instance). So while PDP11
was able to settle into its small manufacturing niche, I am not sure there is
any specific nice for VMS to settle into. "disaster tolerant" isn't a niche.
(disaster tolerant doesn't relate to any specific applications or industry).


But if you look at VMS against its supposed successor (NT), most would still
consider VMS to be way ahead of NT. So many would consider it a downgrade to
move from VMS to NT.

While NT may rule office desktops, it does not mean that all other companies
should actively scuttle their low-end products. Preventing VMS from booting on
low end machines is a bean counter technique to prevent sale of a low end
product instead of a high end one. If those bean counters were kept by Compaq
when it took over Digital, things do not bode well at Compaq. 

The problem with VMS is not so much technical as it is
administrative/philosophical. It is its marketing/pricing which is the
problem. Until NT has implemented all of VMS's core functionalities given to
MS, there is no reason to actively scuttle the operating system and let it die slowly.


Unless, of course, Compaq is fully aware of a death curve of an operating
system when it is overpriced and figures that at VMS's current non-competitive
prices, it can still  make enough revenus for a few years to make it worth it.

What's we'd like to see is a LONG TERM commitment for VMS and this means
consider VMS to be a competitive product and price it so.



