core__dump@my-dejanews.com writes:
> [snip]
> Searching for new leaks in heap after program exit
> 
> 48 bytes in 1 leak (including 1 super leak) created at:
>     malloc                         libc.so
>     _findiop                       libc.so
>     fopen                          libc.so
>     main                           testprog
>     __start                        testprog
> [snip]
> Is there really a memory leak within "fopen"?

It's perfectly possible.  But:

(1) because it's within libc you cannot directly do anything about it
(indirectly you can complain to Digital UNIX product support)

(2) Whether it's a "real" leak is debatable.  Do 1000 fopens and if
you leak 48000 bytes, then you should get worried and complain really
loudly.  But if the leak stays small regardless of the number of
fopens, don't fret over it, it's most probably just some small working
area that libc stdio allocates.

