In article <qagj67.rmg.ln@localhost>, stu@c49395-a.wodhvn1.mi.home.com (Stuart R. Fuller) writes:
|> Boris Gjenero (bgjenero@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca) wrote:
|> : I'm a bit disappointed with this DECstation 5000/125.  My experience
|> : with my DECstation 3100 made me assume that the 5000 would certainly
|> : outperform a 486DX50.  Unfortunately that is not happening.  (I'd really
|> : like to replace the 486DX50 with the DECstation.)
|> : 
|> : When just doing raw computation the DS5000/125 is decent but when doing
|> : more complicated stuff it is quite slow.  For example running
|> : ../configure for bash takes way too long and compiles run noticeably
|> : slower than on the 486DX50.  In fact in some ways my KA650 based
|> : MicroVAX system performs better than the DS5000/125.
|> : 
|> : Is something wrong or was I just expecting too much from the
|> : DS5000/125?  I'm right now running NetBSD-1.3.3 and using gcc... could
|> : either be slowing things down?  

Relative to what?

|> :(Is Ultrix faster?  Does Digital's cc
|> : generate much better code?)

No, and Yes.  

Running several compute-intensive Ultrix binaries on 5000/260s under
both Ultrix and NetBSD's Ultrix emulation show that NetBSD runs the
same binary faster than Ultrix.

The Ultrix compiler is actually the old MipsCo compiler toolchain;
even the old Ultrix 4.2a version produces code that's about 10% faster
than static gcc binaries.  And the cost of the MIPS ELF ABI-calls for
dynamic linking is signficant: 10% or more. That's why the NetBSD
kernel is compiled without them.


|> : BTW.  The DECstation has 48 megs or RAM and decent SCSI drives, so that
|> : is not the problem.
|> 
|> The 5000-100 series runs at 1xx-100 clock speed.  So, your -125 is running at
|> 25 MHz.  Your 486 is running at 50Mhz.  Draw your own conclusions.


Other things being equal, a 25 MHz r3000 should be quite competitive
with a 486DX50.

I think the real performance problem with NetBSD/pmax 1.3.x and
script-based or exec-heavy code is in the VM subsystem.  It still uses
the 4.4BSD VM system, which I've heard described as Mach VM bolted
onto BSD and then held together with a glue gun.  With Chuck Cranor's
UVM VM-system (as in NetBSD-current), exec-heavy workloads like GNU
configure run significantly faster.  Using a gcc that's statically
linked against libcc1 would also help.

Just out of curiosity, what's the OS on the 486DX2?



