It's possible that you can do that with host routing on the Alpha,
setting up static routes for each and every PC (to make sure the
packets get out on the correct interface).  Some table to build...

In such a case, I don't see why you shouldn't do subnets.  Since you
seem to have all of a.b/16 (you do understand CIDR specifications,
yes?), you could divide that into as few as 4 subnetworks (a.b.0/17, 
a.b.64/17, a.b.128/17 and a.b.192/17), or more (but smaller) if you
wish (there was some odity with UCX 4 and subnets, so it's possible
that the subnetworks need to be a.b.c/24), and then you'd only need to
add one extra route per subnetwork.  Much less hassle, especially if
you consider a fluxuating future, with new PC's being added...

However, the following piece of text makes me think I'm guessing wrong.

   My thoughts are that routing will send to the first interface not to the 2
   others - I'm in the process of getting 2 more network adaptors to try
   setting this up and see what it will do.

This is slightly incomprehensible.  It sounds likeyou tell us that you
will only talk through one interface.  Then please tell me why you
need three of them?

