Jonathan Stone wrote:
> |> : Is something wrong or was I just expecting too much from the
> |> : DS5000/125?  I'm right now running NetBSD-1.3.3 and using gcc... could
> |> : either be slowing things down?
> 
> Relative to what?

I was thinking more along the lines of it all being less efficient than
on other platforms and therefore making the DS5000/125 seem unfairly
slow.  (There may be some problems with this statement, so maybe I
should just say "relative to Ultrix")

> |> :(Is Ultrix faster?  Does Digital's cc
> |> : generate much better code?)
> 
> No, and Yes.
> 
> Running several compute-intensive Ultrix binaries on 5000/260s under
> both Ultrix and NetBSD's Ultrix emulation show that NetBSD runs the
> same binary faster than Ultrix.

Interesting... and I'm wondering why this is so.

> The Ultrix compiler is actually the old MipsCo compiler toolchain;
> even the old Ultrix 4.2a version produces code that's about 10% faster
> than static gcc binaries.  And the cost of the MIPS ELF ABI-calls for

Okay.  The compiler is much faster than gcc too.  Configuring and
compiling bash on my DECstation 3100 running Ultrix 4.4 felt just as
fast or even faster than on the 486DX50, and certainly considerably
faster than on the DECstation 5000/125 running NetBSD-1.3.3.

(Hmm... it seems that gcc is good with CISC CPUs like x86 and VAX, but
it sucks with RISC CPUs like Alpha and MIPS.  I wonder if this is a
correct overall observation.)

> Other things being equal, a 25 MHz r3000 should be quite competitive
> with a 486DX50.

Well, apparently it isn't.  It beats the 486 by a significant margin
when doing integer Mandelbrot set computations, but that's probably just
because integer multiplication takes so many cycles (12-42 or something
like that) on the 486.  I haven't tested floating point performance, but
I assume that the DECstation will be good at that too. (Yeah, the
DECstation has a coprocessor)

> I think the real performance problem with NetBSD/pmax 1.3.x and
> script-based or exec-heavy code is in the VM subsystem.  It still uses
> the 4.4BSD VM system, which I've heard described as Mach VM bolted
> onto BSD and then held together with a glue gun.  With Chuck Cranor's
> UVM VM-system (as in NetBSD-current), exec-heavy workloads like GNU
> configure run significantly faster.  Using a gcc that's statically
> linked against libcc1 would also help.

Okay... I guess this means I should try NetBSD-current with the UVM
option and see if that is better.

> Just out of curiosity, what's the OS on the 486DX2?

It's running Linux 2.0.33.

BTW. No, it's not a DX2 it is a real (non-clock-doubled) DX.

