Newsgroups: comp.org.decus,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec
X-Http-User-Agent: Mozilla/2.02 (OS/2; I)

Compaq has chosen a losing strategy when they continue pricing
their operating systems the way Digital did. They say Alpha
architecture, Digital Unix and OpenVMS are getting their full
attention. On my opinion, they are not practising what they
preach.

To make sure Alpha platform has a future Compaq should seriously
reconsider their OS pricing strategy. They need to get aggressive
to keep up the installed base. Sun is giving its Solaris for free
for non-commercial and educational users and SCO has been doing
that for long. As we know, the developers and IT professionals in
commercial world have a non-commercial background, and they are
not going to make a transition from their familiar platforms to
Alpha just for the technical excellence.

Currently, Alpha is not a very tempting platform for a
non-commercial developer because of the price barrier: OpenVMS and
Digital Unix licenses cost a fortune. Windows NT, on the other
hand, does not include libraries and header files, so you cannot
use free compilers. (Microsoft offers quite satisfactory
educational discounts, though.)

What about Linux? Linux is not going to save Alpha by itself. The
vast majority of the Linux society runs PCs. Now it comes to the
"familiar platform" issue again. I know SOME would make the
transition, but very few. Moreover, Linux still lacks many of the
features commercial unices have and is not as robust. This is not
such a big issue in private use, but something that at least the
educational world appreciates very much. NetBSD and OpenBSD are
more stable and secure, but support less hardware.

I purchased a new AlphaStation 600 about a year ago. It came
with DUnix pre-installed, but did not include the license. All
I would need is Open3D to get support for my ZLXp-L2. (One thing
you cannot get with these non-commercial unices is hardware
accelerated OpenGL.) However, the license would have cost more
than I payed for the complete workstation.

Compaq, I am not saying OSes should be free, but they should not
cost more than the hardware.

--
