On Sun, 3 Jan 1999 01:47:24, jmf9@po.cwru.edu (Jon Fiedler) wrote:

> While I can't say that I'd buy a Fortran compiler (mainly because I
> have no use for one), I would like to address the support issue.  I
> seem to be hearing a lot of companies saying that they'd release
> products for Linux except that "everyone's system is different, so
> support  costs would be through the roof" (or something along those
> lines).  What I don't understand is why don't they validate their
> product on certain releases from the main distributions and just say
> "we'll only support you if you're running <blah>".  Then you could
> have some control over what is on their system.

I take your point Jon but I suspect the Linux developer community 
might not like it. Users on the other hand...

Our compamy used TeamWork under VMS for some odd reason (it was not 
the best tool for that particular job,TeamWork that is :-)) but that 
went Digital Unix and then the developers said Sun only. The younger 
bods who did Unix at college like it, us oldies are not so keen on 
being forced to a particular platform. Particulary over half way 
through a project.

As it happens I write in Fortran and may even be using my home Linux 
system to do some development work (for VMS/ELN) while I'm at home. 
(using the Fortran that comes  with it) On the other hand, I'd 
probably be better of with NT Digital Visual Fortran but that's a lot 
of money for a couple of days work per year.  I bought the Microsoft 
one (Powerstation) 'cos it said 'VAX-Fortran  compatible' on the 
advert but, of course, it isn't !! I ought to have known better. M$oft
use a different dictionary to me.

Cheers - Dave.



