On Fri, 1 Jan 1999, Eric Christy wrote:
> Ryan Moore wrote:
> > 4 is the CPU family ID.  4 means a 21064 Alpha chip.  The 21064 is the
> > first-generation of Alpha that was released to consumers.
> >
> > 200 is a model number.  Looks like the big difference between the 200 and the
> > 255 series is that the 255 model series includes 21064a Alphas.  They are
> > slightly faster than 21064 chips.  Mostly because they include more cache
> > memory on chip.
> 
> Actually, the 200 4/233 has a 21064a as I believe the 4/166  does also.  The
> 255 does have a larger L2 cache however.

Well, if the AlphaStation 200 models have 21064a chips, then Digital's
web page is inaccurate.  That's certainly possible.

According to http://www.workstation.digital.com/products/a200/Performance2.html
the AS 200 contains 21064 chips.  The 166 MHz has 8KB/8KB I/D cache.  The
AS200 4/233 has 16KB/16KB I/D.  Both with a 512K L2 cache.

According to http://www.workstation.digital.com/products/a255/performance.html
the AS 255 contains 21064a chips.  Both the 233 and 300 include 16KB/16KB
caches with 1MB L2 cache. 

